# 8. CLI
# ==============================================================================

print_usage() {
    cat <<'EOF'
bili-resolve — the Bilibili resolve engine: a handle becomes bytes (non-interactive)

Usage:
  bili-resolve [-f MODE] [-S SORT] [--quality TIER] [-j|-J] -- <BV id | av id | URL>
  bili-resolve --info [-j|-J] -- <BV id | av id | URL>
  bili-resolve --parts [-j|-J] -- <BV id | av id | URL>
  bili-resolve --items [--cursor TOKEN] [-j|-J] -- <am id | ml id | menu, favlist or
                                                     collection URL>
  bili-resolve --auth [-j]
  bili-resolve --capabilities [-j]

The handle is a Bilibili URL, a BV id (what `bili-search -j` returns as results[].id), or
the legacy av id. Knowing those shapes is this engine's job, not the player's.

Options:
  -f MODE  Playback mode the format is chosen for: audio | video | fast | ascii | viz
           (default: audio). The MODE -> yt-dlp format table lives here, not in t-play.
  -S SORT  yt-dlp format-sort override (--format-sort)
  --quality TIER  Quality tier: auto | low | medium | high (default: auto). The
           (mode, tier) -> format-sort table lives here, not in t-play; -S overrides it
  -j       JSON envelope (see below)
  -J       Full raw yt-dlp record
  --info   Metadata only (title, channel, description) — nothing is resolved
  --parts  List the parts (?p=N) of a multi-part video — one HTTP request, no yt-dlp
           (a second one only when the preferred endpoint refuses; see `title` below).
           Every element is an item record, so the list pipes into t-playlist --add and
           t-play --queue unmapped. A single-part video lists one part, not an error
  --items  List what is in a container — HTTP only, no yt-dlp. Three of them on this site:
           an audio menu (am<id>, /audio/am<id>), a favourites list (ml<id>,
           /medialist/detail/ml<id>, or space…/<uid>/favlist?fid=…) and a collection
           (space…/<uid>/lists/<sid>?type=season, …/channel/collectiondetail?sid=…).
           Every element is an item record, so the list pipes into t-playlist --add and
           t-play --queue unmapped. Up to 500 rows per call (480 for a favourites list,
           which pages in 40s) — has_more/next_cursor say whether there are more and how
           to ask. count below total means the container is longer than one batch, or held
           rows this engine could not play: a dead upload or an OGV episode in a favourites
           list is dropped. An empty container lists 0 items and is not an error, and a
           list this anonymous client cannot see is reported as an empty one
  --cursor TOKEN  Continue an --items listing: pass back the next_cursor of the envelope
           before it. Opaque, and the same shape in every engine of this suite
  --auth   Report the cookie decision: which browser profile playback will read, and
           whether it exists here. No network, no handle. Says cookies are SENT, not
           that they are accepted — an expired login still reports "cookie"
  --capabilities  The flags this half accepts, less the -l --color -h -V every command
           has. No network, no handle, no dependency: how a caller learns what to ask
  --color MODE  auto | always | never (default: auto)
  -h       This help
  -V       Print the suite version

Resolve JSON (-j): {"status","engine","id","url","title","duration","mode","format",
                    "selected","selected_resolution",
                    "stream_urls":[...],"http_headers":{...},"retried"}
  format is the selection string SENT; selected is the format yt-dlp actually PICKED, and
  selected_resolution its resolution ("audio only" when there is no video).
  Identical to yt-resolve's envelope, key for key.
  http_headers is REQUIRED and on this site it MATTERS: the CDN checks Referer, and a bare
  stream URL answers 403 where the same URL with these headers answers 206.

Info JSON (--info -j): {status,engine,id,title,url,channel,uploader,upload_date,duration,
                        duration_fmt,view_count,like_count,live_status,description,chapters}

Parts JSON (--parts -j): {status,engine,id,title,url,count,total_duration,
                          total_duration_fmt,parts:[{n,engine,url,title,duration,
                                                     duration_fmt}]}
  parts[] elements ARE item records:  --parts -j | jq '{items:.parts}' | t-playlist --add …
  Top-level `title` is null when the fallback endpoint answered — it does not carry the
  collection title. Every part's own title, number, duration and url are there either way,
  and an av handle keeps its av spelling in `id`/`url` instead of being canonicalised to BV.
  The verb needs an id, so a b23.tv short link (a redirect) is refused — pass the BV id.
  There is no --parts on yt-resolve: one id there is one file, and its many-item form is a
  playlist under its own URL. A caller probes for the verb by invoking it with no handle.

Items JSON (--items -j): {status,engine,id,url,title,count,total,has_more,next_cursor,
                          items:[{n,engine,id,url,title,duration,duration_fmt}]}
  count is what came back; total is what the site says the container holds. They differ when
  it is longer than one batch or when a row was not playable, and the envelope does not
  say which — the fix for either is the same, ask for less or accept less.
  has_more says whether the CONTAINER holds rows beyond this batch (never whether rows were
  filtered out of it), and next_cursor is the token to ask for them with — null exactly when
  has_more is false. n numbers the rows of THIS batch, from 1. An audio menu's records
  name tracks (au ids), a favourites list's and a
  collection's name videos (BV ids) — both the way resolving one of them answers.
  -J returns the body that carried the rows for THIS batch, every page of it merged into one;
  paging is something -j can do and -J cannot, the cursor keys being envelope keys.
  A container is NOT a search row: a menu has no duration, and its track count and cursor
  have nowhere to live on a row. That is why this verb exists instead of a `kind`.

Auth JSON (--auth -j): {status,engine,auth,cookie_browser,profile_found,cookie_readable}
  auth is "cookie" exactly when cookie_browser is not "none" AND profile_found.
  cookie_readable: can this process read the store (true|false; null = not tried/no probe).
Capabilities JSON (--capabilities -j): {status,engine,flags[]}

There is no --transcript: this site's videos carry no caption track for yt-dlp to fetch
(measured: subtitles {} and automatic_captions absent), and a verb that always answers
"none" is worse than a verb that says it does not exist.

A multi-part video resolves to PART ONE. Most music results here are multi-hour
collections, so this is the useful default; to pick another part, pass the URL with ?p=N —
or ask --parts for the whole list, which is where those URLs come from.

Exit codes: 0 ok · 1 usage/validation · 2+ a missing dependency, or a propagated
            yt-dlp failure (see .reason)

Environment:
  Defaults for every knob below live in <checkout>/config, declared once for the whole
  suite. Override per user in ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/ting/config (KEY=value, one
  per line, # comments; the pre-rename .../uting/config is read when there is no ting
  one); TING_CONFIG relocates it, and UT_CONFIG still does. Chain: flag > environment > your
  config > the shipped defaults. See ARCH-cli-contract.md「配置面」.
  BILI_COOKIE_BROWSER      Browser profile cookies are read from (default: chrome;
                           "none" for anonymous). On this site login buys audio QUALITY —
                           an account is offered renditions an anonymous client is not.
  BILI_AUDIO_FORMAT        Format for audio/viz modes (default: ba/b)
  BILI_VIDEO_FORMAT        Format for video/ascii modes (default: bv*+ba/b)
  BILI_VIDEO_FORMAT_FAST   Format for fast mode
  BILI_UA                  User-Agent sent with the --parts requests (a browser UA; the
                           site is known to start refusing one that has gone stale). It is
                           the ONLY verb here that sends a request of its own making —
                           every other one goes through yt-dlp, which carries its own.

To search: bili-search <query>   ·   To play: t-play --engine bili <BV id>
EOF
}

# Every flag this half takes beyond the -l --color -h -V every command has, stated ONCE: the
# unknown-flag refusal prints it for a person and --capabilities answers with it for a caller,
# so the two cannot disagree about what this engine has (ARCH-cli-contract.md「命令规格」).
RESOLVE_FLAGS=(-f -S --quality -j -J --info --parts --items --cursor --auth --capabilities)

has_format=0
positional=()
while (($#)); do
    case "$1" in
    -h | --help)
        print_usage
        exit 0
        ;;
    -V | --version)
        # Answered before ANY dependency gate: a version is the first thing a bug report
        # needs, and needing yt-dlp installed to find out which version you have is
        # backwards.
        printf 'bili-resolve %s\n' "$UT_VERSION"
        exit 0
        ;;
    -j | --json)
        has_format=1
        OUTPUT_MODE="json"
        shift
        ;;
    -J | --json-full)
        has_format=1
        OUTPUT_MODE="json_full"
        shift
        ;;
    -l | --list)
        has_format=1
        OUTPUT_MODE="list"
        shift
        ;;
    --info)
        set_action info --info
        shift
        ;;
    --parts)
        set_action parts --parts
        shift
        ;;
    --items)
        set_action items --items
        shift
        ;;
    --cursor=*)
        CURSOR="${1#*=}"
        shift
        ;;
    --cursor)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "bili-resolve: --cursor requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        CURSOR="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    --auth)
        set_action auth --auth
        shift
        ;;
    --capabilities)
        set_action capabilities --capabilities
        shift
        ;;
    # Named rather than swept up by the unknown-flag arm: a caller reaching for captions is
    # holding a reasonable expectation (the sibling engine has them), and the useful answer
    # is why this site cannot serve it — not "unknown flag".
    --transcript | --subtitles | --sub-lang | --sub-langs | --sub-lang=* | --sub-langs=*)
        echo "bili-resolve: this site's videos carry no caption track — there is no ${1%%=*} verb here" >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
    --color=*)
        COLOR_MODE="${1#*=}"
        shift
        ;;
    --color)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "bili-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        COLOR_MODE="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    -f | --format-mode)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "bili-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        FORMAT_FLAG="$1"
        # Canonical modes only — the alias table (ba/bv/…) is t-play's, which normalizes
        # before it calls an engine (ARCH-cli-contract.md「命令规格」).
        validate_enum "$2" audio video fast ascii viz ||
            die "-f must be one of: audio, video, fast, ascii, viz"
        PLAYBACK_MODE="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    -S)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "bili-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        FORMAT_FLAG="$1"
        FORMAT_SORT="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    --quality)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "bili-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        FORMAT_FLAG="$1"
        # Canonical tiers only — the alias table, if one ever grows one, is t-play's
        # (the same rule as -f: the engine seam carries canonical spellings).
        validate_enum "$2" auto low medium high ||
            die "--quality must be one of: auto, low, medium, high"
        QUALITY="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    # The gate. Resolution neither plays nor tracks a player, and it does not search:
    # a caller holding the wrong verb is told which one it wants (ARCH-cli-contract.md「门模型」).
    -d | --detach | --status | --stop | --set-volume | --id | --all | --volume)
        echo "bili-resolve: $1 is a playback flag — use t-play for playback" >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
    -n | -m | -M | -s)
        echo "bili-resolve: $1 is a search flag — use bili-search for searching" >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
    --)
        shift
        # `--` stops FLAG parsing, not argument validation: a handle beginning with '-'
        # must survive, and a second handle is still an error. ARCHITECTURE.md「端到端控制流」.
        while (($#)); do
            positional+=("$1")
            shift
        done
        break
        ;;
    -*)
        echo "bili-resolve: unknown flag '$1' (resolve flags: ${RESOLVE_FLAGS[*]})" >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
    *)
        positional+=("$1")
        shift
        ;;
    esac
done

# --auth asks about the ENGINE, not about a handle, so it is the one verb here that takes
# no positional — and a handle passed with it is rejected rather than ignored, the same way
# -f next to --info is (ARCH-cli-contract.md「门模型」).
TARGET=""
if [[ "$ACTION" == "auth" ]]; then
    if ((${#positional[@]} > 0)); then
        echo "bili-resolve: --auth takes no argument — it reports this engine's cookie decision, not a handle's" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
elif [[ "$ACTION" == "capabilities" ]]; then
    if ((${#positional[@]} > 0)); then
        echo "bili-resolve: --capabilities takes no argument — it reports what this engine accepts, not anything about a handle" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
else
    # --items wants a CONTAINER, so it says so: a caller handed the "BV id" sentence while
    # asking for a menu would go looking for the wrong handle.
    if ((${#positional[@]} == 0)); then
        if [[ "$ACTION" == "items" ]]; then
            echo "bili-resolve: an am id or an audio menu URL is required (see 'bili-resolve -h')" >&2
        else
            echo "bili-resolve: a BV id, av id or URL is required (see 'bili-resolve -h')" >&2
        fi
        exit 1
    fi
    if ((${#positional[@]} > 1)); then
        if [[ "$ACTION" == "items" ]]; then
            echo "bili-resolve: takes exactly one am id or audio menu URL (got ${#positional[@]})" >&2
        else
            echo "bili-resolve: takes exactly one BV id, av id or URL (got ${#positional[@]})" >&2
        fi
        exit 1
    fi
    TARGET="${positional[0]}"
fi

# The default output is prose; -j/-J/-l override.
((has_format)) || OUTPUT_MODE="list"

if ! validate_enum "$COLOR_MODE" auto always never; then
    die "--color must be one of: auto, always, never"
fi
# THE CURSOR IS A TOKEN THIS ENGINE MINTED, so both ways of getting it wrong are answered here,
# before a packet is spent: it belongs to --items (no other verb has batches), and it has one
# shape. The shape is the same in all three engines — `o:` and an item offset — so a cursor
# handed to the wrong engine is caught in argv instead of a request later
# (ARCH-engine.md「容器（--items）」).
if [[ -n "$CURSOR" ]]; then
    [[ "$ACTION" == "items" ]] || die "--cursor continues an --items listing — pass --items"
    [[ "$CURSOR" =~ ^o:[0-9]+$ ]] ||
        die "'$CURSOR' is not a cursor — pass back the next_cursor an --items envelope handed you"
    CURSOR_OFFSET="${CURSOR#o:}"
fi

# -f / -S pick a FORMAT, and only stream resolution resolves one. Next to --info they
# would be accepted and quietly do nothing, which is precisely what a gating layer exists
# to reject rather than ignore (ARCH-cli-contract.md「门模型」).
if [[ -n "$FORMAT_FLAG" && "$ACTION" != "stream" ]]; then
    die "$FORMAT_FLAG selects a stream format — it does not apply to --$ACTION"
fi

# -J is the RAW yt-dlp record, and neither --auth nor --capabilities runs yt-dlp: there is
# no raw record for it to return. Rejected rather than silently downgraded to -j.
if [[ ( "$ACTION" == "auth" || "$ACTION" == "capabilities" ) && "$OUTPUT_MODE" == "json_full" ]]; then
    die "-J returns the raw yt-dlp record — --$ACTION runs no yt-dlp; use -j"
fi

# --capabilities answers ahead of every gate that needs a tool, jq included: it is how a caller
# learns what to ask this engine, so it must answer on a machine that cannot run any of it yet.
# The flags are fixed ASCII words, so the envelope is printed rather than built.
if [[ "$ACTION" == "capabilities" ]]; then
    if [[ "$OUTPUT_MODE" == "json" ]]; then
        caps=""
        for f in "${RESOLVE_FLAGS[@]}"; do caps+="${caps:+,}\"$f\""; done
        printf '{"status":"ok","engine":"%s","flags":[%s]}\n' "$ENGINE_NAME" "$caps"
    else
        printf '%s\n' "${RESOLVE_FLAGS[*]}"
    fi
    exit 0
fi

# --auth answers ahead of the dependency gate and the handle grammar, the way -V does: it
# reports configuration, so it must not need the tool it is reporting the configuration of.
if [[ "$ACTION" == "auth" ]]; then
    [[ "$OUTPUT_MODE" == "json" ]] && require_deps jq
    resolve_auth
    exit 0
fi

# --items has its own grammar and its own transport, and it is dispatched BEFORE
# normalize_target for both reasons: a menu handle is not a video handle, and the stream
# verb's grammar would either refuse it or hand it to an extractor that answers with an
# empty stream list. curl fetches and jq parses, so the extractor is not this verb's
# dependency either — the same lazy gate --parts and --auth already get.
if [[ "$ACTION" == "items" ]]; then
    normalize_container "$TARGET"
    require_deps curl jq
    resolve_items
    exit 0
fi

normalize_target "$TARGET"

# --parts runs NO yt-dlp — it is one HTTP request, or two when the first is refused — so
# requiring the extractor for it would be the same backwards gate --auth refuses above.
# curl is this verb's dependency and jq
# parses what comes back; both are already required elsewhere in the engine pair.
if [[ "$ACTION" == "parts" ]]; then
    require_deps curl jq
    resolve_parts
    exit 0
fi

require_deps yt-dlp jq

case "$ACTION" in
info) resolve_info ;;
*) resolve_stream ;;
esac
exit 0
